# Supabase .env delta

The API now depends on the Supabase credentials outlined in `docs/supabase-auth-plan.md` (see the **Supabase Studio Configuration & Keys** table). Make sure every `.env`-style file contains the following keys:

- `SUPABASE_URL` – The project URL (e.g., `https://<project-ref>.supabase.co`) used whenever we instantiate Supabase clients.
- `SUPABASE_ANON_KEY` – Publishable key that the React Native client uses for sign-up/sign-in flows.
- `SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY` – Server-only key that powers privileged calls, middleware validation, and background jobs.
- `SUPABASE_JWT_SECRET` – Only needed when minting/validating HS256 tokens ourselves; keep it private.
- `SUPABASE_JWKS_URL` – Usually `${SUPABASE_URL}/auth/v1/jwks`; the Express middleware hits this endpoint via `jose.createRemoteJWKSet`.

These entries already exist in `.env`; mirror the placeholder values inside `.env.example` whenever onboarding a new environment.

## Chat API

- `IMOTKO_WEB_URL` – Canonical public URL of the existing Next.js application, such as `https://imotko.mk`. Express uses it only for chat dashboard links and Supabase magic-link redirects.
- `UPSTASH_REDIS_URL` – Optional Redis connection URL shared by the API. It enforces guest account-creation limits; a Redis outage deliberately fails open and is logged.
- `AWS_SES_KEY`, `AWS_SES_SECRET_KEY`, `AWS_SES_REGION`, and `IMOTKO_EMAIL` – Existing SES settings used for immediate chat new-message email. The existing Next.js application remains the sole unread-reminder scheduler.
- `EXPO_ACCESS_TOKEN` – Optional server-only Expo access token. It becomes required after Enhanced Push Security is enabled for the Expo project.
